{"id":"b9e8b211-a4b7-4340-be0b-cef459a42895","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885485,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import heapq\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    removed = set()\\n    heap = [(-n['depth'], n['id']) for n in nodes]\\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while heap:\\n        neg_depth, nid = heapq.heappop(heap)\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        leaf = all(c not in removed for c in children[nid])\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold and leaf:\\n            removed.add(nid)\\n            pid = node['parent']\\n            if pid is not None:\\n                children[pid].remove(nid)\\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-node_map[pid]['depth'], pid))\\n    retained = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\\n    return {\\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained, \\\"visit_order\\\": visit_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem. The task is SE-PE-02, which is about a tree reduction queue. We are given a case payload with nodes (each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and a utility_threshold. The objective: \"Maintain and update the syntax-guided reduction work queue across accepted and rejected tree rewrites without revisiting invalidated nodes.\"\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) that returns {\"retained_node_ids\": [...], \"visit_order\": [...]}. The algorithm:\n\n- Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\n- Skip nodes already removed.\n- Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\n- After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\n- Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\n\nInterpretation: We have a tree. We process nodes in order of descending depth, and for same depth, stable (presumably alphabetical or arbitrary but consistent) node-ID order. For each node in queue, we check if it is still present (not removed), if it is a leaf (i.e., it has no children that remain), and if it is removable (removable==true) and its utility <= utility_threshold. If all conditions, we remove it (mark as removed) and enqueue its parent (if parent is not null) for reconsideration. The parent might need to be re-evaluated because now it might become a leaf after child removal. We continue processing the queue. The queue order is initially descending depth and stable ID, but as we add parent nodes to the queue, we need to insert them in the correct order? The description: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" Then \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" It doesn't say where in the queue to enqueue the parent. But typical algorithm might be to add to the end, or maintain priority order. However, \"queue_order\" is a hard contract, so we need to determine the exact expected behavior. The problem statement: \"queue_order\" as a hard contract. Probably the queue should always be processed in descending depth and stable ID order. So when we enqueue a parent, we should insert it at appropriate position based on depth (descending) and then ID. But the parent's depth is less than the removed node's depth, so it will be placed before (or after?) because descending depth: larger depth first. The parent depth is smaller, so it would be later in the queue (since we process from highest depth to lowest). Actually descending depth means we start with deepest nodes. So after processing all nodes at depth D, we move to D-1. If we remove a node and enqueue its parent (depth D-1), then that parent should be considered at the appropriate time among other nodes at its depth. Since we are currently at depth D or maybe D-1, we need to insert it in the queue. The simple approach: maintain a priority queue where key is (-depth, node_id) to process in descending depth then ascending ID (stable). When we enqueue parent, push it onto priority queue. That ensures we always process in that order. But the initial queue also should be that order. And we only process nodes that are still present and not removed. We should skip removed nodes when popped.\n\nAlso \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So when we pop a node from queue, if it's marked removed, we skip it.\n\n\"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" So we check if node is removable, utility <= threshold, and it has no children that are still present (i.e., not removed). Leaves are nodes with no remaining children. Since tree is given, we need to maintain child sets. Initially build adjacency list parent->children. When removing a node, we remove it from its parent's children list.\n\n\"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" Since parent is retained (i.e., not removed), we add to queue.\n\n\"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" The visit order should be the order in which nodes were processed (i.e., popped from queue and considered). Not just removals. The queue order is the order we process. So we need to record the node IDs in the order they are popped from the queue (and not skipped because removed). But \"complete visit order\" might include all nodes visited (including those that were not removed). That seems plausible.\n\nEdge cases: root may be not removable (removable: false). So root can never be removed. Also if a node is not removable, it cannot be removed even if leaf and utility low.\n\nAlgorithm steps for solve(case):\n\n1. Extract nodes list and utility_threshold from case.\n2. Build a dict node_by_id.\n3. Build children dict: for each node, children list initially from parent references.\n   - Also store parent ID for each node (except root).\n4. Initialize a set of removed nodes empty.\n5. Initialize a priority queue. For each node, push (-depth, node_id). Use heapq.\n   - But ensure stable order for same depth: using node_id string comparison. Python's heap will compare tuples, and strings compare lexicographically. That is stable and as per \"stable node-ID order\" likely ascending order (alphabetical). So (-depth, node_id) will give descending depth then ascending ID.\n6. Initialize visit_order list.\n7. While queue not empty:\n   - Pop (-depth, node_id). Actually we push tuple, so pop returns it. Convert depth back to positive.\n   - If node_id is in removed: continue.\n   - Add node_id to visit_order.\n   - Get node object.\n   - Determine if node is leaf: check children list for that node (stored separately). Leaf if len(children[node_id]) == 0.\n   - If node.removable and node.utility <= utility_threshold and leaf:\n        - Remove node: mark removed (add to removed set).\n        - For each child in children[node_id], we should remove them from parent? Actually we are removing the node, so its children become orphan? But children should also be removed? The description only says \"remove only a removable leaf\". If we remove a leaf node, it has no children, so children list is empty anyway. But what if a node becomes leaf later after its children are removed? That's fine. For removal of a leaf, we only need to update its parent's children list.\n        - Parent = node.parent (if not None). Then we need to remove node_id from children[parent] list.\n        - Then enqueue parent for reconsideration: push (-parent_depth, parent_id) into heap, only if parent is not removed and not already in queue? But we can always push again; duplicates will be skipped when popped if node already removed. But to avoid infinite loops, we should not reprocess if already processed? Actually it's okay to push multiple times; we skip if removed. However, we might process the parent multiple times before it's removed? But parent may be considered again after each child removal. That's intended. However, we must ensure we don't keep enqueuing the same parent multiple times in a loop without removal. But each time a child is removed, parent is enqueued, and when popped, if it's a leaf and removable, it can be removed. That's fine.\n8. After loop, retained_node_ids are those not in removed set.\n9. Return {\"retained_node_ids\": list(retained ids in any order? The schema just says array of strings. Probably order doesn't matter, but we could sort them consistently. But the contract doesn't specify order. We'll return them in the order of node IDs from original list? That's fine. Or we can just list all node ids not in removed. Since root is always retained.\n10. Visit order: the order we processed (popped) nodes that were not removed. That is the complete queue order.\n\nTest with given examples? We can manually simulate first case to ensure logic.\n\nFirst case payload:\nnodes:\nroot (depth0, not removable)\nn1 (depth1, not removable, utility 0.077)\nn2 (depth1, removable, utility 0.41)\nn3 (depth1, not removable, utility 0.463)\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, removable, utility 0.177)\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, not removable, utility 0.928)\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, removable, utility 0.332)\n\nutility_threshold: 0.55\n\nInitial queue: processing descending depth, ascending ID: depth2 nodes: n4 (depth2, id=\"n4\"), n5 (n5), n6 (n6) order: \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\" (since alphabetical). Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. Then depth0: root.\n\nLet's simulate:\n\nQueue initial push all non-removed? We push all nodes initially. But root removable false, but still push. OK.\n\nPop n4: depth2, leaf? n4's children? none, so leaf. Removable true, utility 0.177 <= 0.55, so remove n4. Mark removed. Parent = n2. Remove n4 from children[n2]. Enqueue n2: push (-1, \"n2\").\n\nPop n5: depth2, leaf (no children), not removable (removable false), so not removed. Visit order: n5. Do not enqueue parent.\n\nPop n6: depth2, leaf, removable true, utility 0.332 <= 0.55, so remove n6. Parent = n1. Remove n6 from children[n1]. Enqueue n1: push (-1, \"n1\").\n\nNow queue has: n1, n2 (both depth1) and the depth1 nodes already there? Actually initial depth1 nodes: n1, n2, n3 were pushed initially, so they are still in heap. But we also pushed n1 and n2 again. So heap will have duplicates.\n\nPop next: smallest tuple: (-1, \"n1\") because \"n1\" < \"n2\". We pop one of the n1 entries. n1 is not removed. Visit order: n1. Check leaf? n1 originally had child n6, but n6 removed, so children[n1] = [] now, leaf. n1 is not removable (removable false), so not removed.\n\nPop next: (-1, \"n2\") (first occurrence). n2 not removed. Visit order: n2. Leaf? n2 had child n4, but n4 removed, so children[n2] = [] now, leaf. n2 removable true, utility 0.41 <= 0.55, so remove n2. Mark removed. Parent = root. Remove n2 from children[root]. Enqueue root: push (-0, \"root\").\n\nPop next: (-1, \"n3\") (original depth1 node). n3 not removed. Visit order: n3. Leaf? n3 has child n5 (not removed), so children[n3] = [\"n5\"], not leaf. So not removed.\n\nPop next: (-0, \"root\") (from enqueue). root not removed. Visit order: root. Leaf? root has children: n1, n3 (since n2 removed). It has children, not leaf. Not removable false anyway.\n\nContinue: eventually all nodes visited. After processing, removed nodes: n4, n6, n2. Retained: root, n1, n3, n5.\n\nVisit order: [\"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\", \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", \"root\"] (exact order we popped). That seems plausible.\n\nCheck second fixture? We'll trust algorithm.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) with these steps.\n\nEdge cases: duplicates in queue: when we pop, we skip removed nodes. Also we may push a node multiple times, but that's fine.\n\nImplementation details: Use heapq. For children, we could use a dict mapping node_id to list of child ids. Initially build from parent fields. Also need to quickly update children when removing a child.\n\nWe'll also need a set of removed nodes.\n\nFor initial queue, we push all nodes (including root). That's fine.\n\nReturn retained_node_ids: we can collect all ids not in removed set. But order? Not specified, but we can return them in the order of the original nodes list to be deterministic. Or sort alphabetically. I'll just use [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] not in removed].\n\nVisit order: list of strings in order we added.\n\nImportant: We must not mutate case. We'll work with copy of data if necessary.\n\nNow, produce Python source code for solve function. Then wrap it in JSON with key \"implementation\". Ensure proper escaping.\n\nAlso note the instruction: \"Do not use Markdown code fences. Do not add a preamble, explanation, or trailing text.\" So output just the JSON object.\n\nLet's construct the JSON.\n\nWe'll write the solve function as:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    import heapq\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    # Build tree\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    removed = set()\n    # Initialize priority queue with all nodes: (-depth, id)\n    heap = [(-n['depth'], n['id']) for n in nodes]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    visit_order = []\n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, nid = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        # Check if leaf: no children that are not removed\n        leaf = all(c not in removed for c in children[nid])\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold and leaf:\n            # Remove this node\n            removed.add(nid)\n            parent_id = node['parent']\n   